  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nSW Flow is in place across the Northern Plains head of a\nstronger mid/upper trough/upper level low located over the \nNorthern Rockies. This upper low tracks east and deepens over \nthe Canadian Prairies and passes north of our region through \nTuesday. There is more variation in details beyond Tuesday \nregarding any shortwave trough passages and rain \ncoverage/chances, though we still remain on the warmer side of \ntemperatures despite a shift towards west-northwest flow on the \nback side of this trough. The general trend is for a much more \nsubstantial shift in flow aloft favoring decreasing heights and \ntemperatures by the end of next week and next weekend (highs in \nthe 60s). The most likely periods of impactful weather will be \nassociated with the trough passage tonight and Tuesday \n(thunderstorms then wind).\n\n...Severe Thunderstorm and Heavy Rain Risk...\n\nOngoing showers and embedded thunderstorms have remain weaker in\na much more elevated airmass featuring skinny CAPE profiles \nbut strong effective shear. Current activity developed with the \nnose of a LLJ and elevated WAA in the 850-700 MB layer and \nbesides heavy rain/lighting it may only feature a marginal hail \nthreat. Expansive cloud cover is also lingering, which may lower\nour window for surface based thunderstorms during the afternoon\nand evening. \n\nSurface pattern indicates surface troughing over western ND, with \nseveral fronts intersecting near Dickinson. The deepening of this \nsurface low and position/timing of these fronts before sunset\nwill be critical for any potential supercell development to \nour west that may track into the Devils Lake Basin/eastern ND \nduring the evening hours. Forcing is much more diffuse before \nthe main trough starts shifting east later this \nevening/overnight, so coverage/initiation carries more \nuncertain. Still, within the warm sector where clearing develops\ndecent instability profiles may coincide with strongly veered \nhodographs/strong shear favorable of supercells and a \nconditional risk for hail around the size of golf balls and a \nconditions risk for tornado development. Some CAMs (mainly runs \nof the HRRR) have supported supercell developing to our west and\npotentially tracking east into our west. The farther east and \nthe later activity lingers/tracks activity will tend to become \nweaker with more of a marginal hail risk.\n\nHigher PWATs in place raises potential for heavy rain rates with \nthunderstorm activity once again as with previous days, and any \nslower moving/training clusters could lead to localized\nflooding.",
